A||N||37||superXmotorsports||squealing pulley/belt||||5
Z||000000||superXmotorsports||11-07-1999||10:49 PM||||I remember a thread not too long ago about a squealing pulley or belt when the engine is cold.  I have had cars with this before and have one doing it now.  What was the diagnosis?  Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||fierospeeder||11-07-1999||11:13 PM||||two causes i know, but i havn't seen the post is, loose belt, worn out belt or the belt has some grease on it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bruce||11-08-1999||12:58 AM||||Mine did the same thing until I had the belt tightened.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Thomas||11-08-1999||08:27 AM||||In my belt i found some little stones at the inner side. (they ones from new streets)||||reg||10
Z||000004||RICK||11-08-1999||09:03 AM||||Mine was just doing that, I'll I had to do was tighten up the alt belt.  Easy fix||||reg||1
Z||000005||superXmotorsports||11-08-1999||09:45 AM||||Thanks for your replies.  I'll try to tighten the belt.||||reg||7
Z||000006||JIM GOOSE||11-10-1999||09:23 AM||||I've noticed that I get a short squealing interval after it has rained on the car when first cranked. This is due to water on the pulleys which are right below the rt. side air vent.  If you get squealing every time, you definitely have a loose belt.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||11-10-1999||09:45 AM||||If it is only the occasional squeal, one might try carefully putting a parrifin wax bar on the belt while the car is running.<P><B>YOU HAVE TO BE VERY CAREFUL IF YOU DO THIS</B><P>But tightening or replacement of the belt are the two best ways to prevent a recurring squealing problem.||||reg||1
Z||000008||WGraffiKs||11-10-1999||05:00 PM||||Why would you put wax on the belt?  Wouldn't that cause it to slip MORE? (But make it quieter until it burned through?)  And lastly, assuming you have a good response to the other two questions, which side of the belt would you put the wax on?||||reg||5
Z||000009||dogtired||11-10-1999||05:20 PM||||That should bring back some memories! But it used to be common practice to use a bar of soap. Start the motor and very carefully put the bar of soap against the generator belt (yes I did say generator) and it would stop making noise for a while And you are absolutely correct, the only thing it does is quite the squeel. (The belt still needs tightened). That is an old timers trick kind-of like putting sawdust in the tranny to quiet it down. Never did that one, but I did use soap on the belts.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||11-10-1999||06:51 PM||||I've never heard of the sawdust in the tranny. What was the thinking behind that one? I'd heard of the soap/parafin before, never had the problem, so I've never tried it.  The belts on these cars are in some of the most remote places I've seen. On my old Sable the alternator was right up on top, very easy to get to the belts.  On my Fiero, those belts are low and lowest.  To change them do you have to pull the inner fender wall/splashguard?||||reg||14
Z||000011||Patrick||11-10-1999||07:38 PM||||<BR>Not too sure about sawdust in the tranny. I would think that the syncros might have a problem with that. Actually, the old trannys didn't have syncros. Maybe it would work. Sawdust in the rear differential is what the (real) old guys used to tell me about.<P><B>fiero56</B>, sawdust in the rear differential would simply take up the slack between the ring and pinion gears and probably the bearings to help quiet everything down.||||reg||3
Z||000012||ggarsk||11-10-1999||07:56 PM||||Saw dust in the tranny helped tighten it up. It's an old used car trick when trying to sell it. It doesn't do much for the life of the tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000013||DJRice||11-11-1999||10:09 AM||||My dad showed me the wax bar trick a long time ago when I had a noisy '78 oldsmobile.<BR>And it does just put off the problem instead of solve it.  But sometimes people are just looking for a quick fix until they can go get new belt to put on.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Phil||11-11-1999||05:48 PM||||Maybe some of the other old guys will remember putting Dutch Cleanser down the carb to help seat the rings on a rebuild.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Tim||11-11-1999||10:29 PM||||That's a new one on me.  I've done the soap on the belts, heard of sawdust in the crankcase, I've put brake fluid in an automatic to get home(swells the cluches and bands to reduce slipping). But never cleanser down the intake. I'll ask my dad, he is old, he's a great-grandfather.||||reg||1
