Z||000026||batboy||11-15-1999||02:29 PM||||If the squealing is actually from the belt, it's either too loose or worn out or both, like was mention early on. But, alternator, water pump, or A/C compressor bearings can squeal too.||||reg||1
Z||000027||theogre||11-15-1999||06:54 PM||||BTW.... It's an egg white in the radiator, not a whole egg. ||||reg||1
Z||000028||superXmotorsports||11-15-1999||07:27 PM||||The scary thing is how many other things did someone try before they came up with these household solutions??||||reg||1
Z||000029||mutfish||11-15-1999||09:51 PM||||I've heard of guys adding mothballs to their fuel to supposedly boost the octane rating.That's been awhile back however.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Tim||11-15-1999||09:57 PM||||I forgot the mothball trick, it did seem to work.  <BR>How about anti-freeze in tires to balance, cool, seal and stop wheel corrosion.  ||||reg||1
Z||000031||theogre||11-15-1999||10:24 PM||||The moth balls work because they are/were made from nearly 100% Naptha. If you use other kinds you could really scrag the motor or fuel system.||||reg||1
Z||000032||olympic||11-15-1999||11:32 PM||||I've heard of the mothball trick but I thought it was only for diesels. Anyways here's 2 more. Slowly Pour a quart of hot water down the carb while someone revs the engine to keep it from stalling. The water hits the hot pistons and explodes into steam, steam-cleaning the combustion chamber.(I wouldn't try this on a fuel injected engine!!!) Also, you can use a set of panty hose as an emergency fan belt.||||reg||3
