Z||000034||Bugsy||11-16-1999||11:15 AM||||sorry for being so late getting into this thread guys but i'm having a problem with my serp. belt squeeling on cold starts, also along with the squeeling the alt. is discharging. any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000035||DJRice||11-17-1999||11:10 AM||||Well either the belts need to be tightened or they need to be replaced.  The alt output goes down because the pulley isnt spinning when as the belt turns around it.  Once the belt begins to spin the alt pulley, the alt provides electricity, and your gauge will show a normal output (assuming your alternator works properly)||||reg||1
Z||000036||Bugsy||11-20-1999||07:36 AM||||i was thinking the alternator or the serpentine belt, and maybe even the belt tensioner....i forgot to mention that when cold i have to turn off the heater when dropping rpm's at stop signs and such...also the alternator is almost over charging when it is charging...the guage is right on the red line....the motor has to come out in the next couple of months for broken manifold bolts anyway...be a good time to replace the serp belt and the alternator ||||reg||1
Z||000037||2birds||11-21-1999||12:31 AM||||I've been fighting belt squeal on my 86 GT after it rains for years.  The best belt I've found is the Goodyear Gatorback.  Think of it like going from slicks to new tires.  It's under $10 from Summit.  Lately I had a leak from my harmonic balancer to timing cover seal.  The seal wore grooves into the balancer.  It was slinging oil all over the place, causing squeal ever time I had the lights on or hit the brakes.  I got a Fel-pro balancer sleeve kit and replaced the timing cover to balancer seal, all for under $15.  It's been two weeks now, and no oil or squeal.  I've also seen a leaky water pump seal, thermostat housing, and hoses cause this, too.  If you see a lot of fluid, but you're not sure where the leak is, try some flourescent tracer dye from a parts store or dealer.  Hope this helps.  ||||reg||1
